abstract: >-
  HelioFleet is a web tool that shows where heliophysics spacecraft (SDO,
  SOHO, Aditya-L1, ACE, Wind, DSCOVR, IMAP, SOLAR-1, Carruthers, Solar
  Orbiter, Parker Solar Probe,
  BepiColombo, STEREO-A/B and MAVEN) are relative to the Sun and Earth on
  any date since 2010. It reports Stonyhurst and Carrington coordinates and
  Parker-spiral footpoints, searches for radial alignments, quadratures and
  magnetic connections, checks which observers see a given point on the Sun,
  and exports coordinates and time series. It draws the fields of view of
  coronagraphs and heliospheric imagers, summarises which solar longitudes
  are imaged on a solar surface map over SDO/HMI synoptic magnetograms, and
  shows flares, CMEs and geomagnetic storms from NASA DONKI. A viewing-geometry
  table gives each observer's light-time offset from Earth, apparent solar
  radius, B0 and L0. A sky view projects every instrument's field of view onto
  the plane of the sky seen from Earth or from a chosen spacecraft, with MLSO
  K-Cor and UCoMP fields, and draws the observation footprints of DKIST, IRIS,
  Hinode/EIS and Solar Orbiter (EUI-HRI, PHI-HRT, SPICE) over SDO/AIA and HMI
  images, with a finder for times when chosen facilities observed the same
  place together. Positions come from JPL Horizons ephemerides, refreshed
  monthly. The interface is available in English, Hindi and Spanish.
keywords:
  - heliophysics
  - spacecraft ephemerides
  - solar physics
  - multi-spacecraft observations
  - solar eclipse
